Task 5: Constraints, Formalisation and Violations — Railway Crossing
5.1 Ten safety constraints
For this task, assume the following Boolean variables are monitored:
- Train_Present: a train is detected or approaching.
- Barrier_Open, Barrier_Closed: confirmed physical barrier positions.
- Robot_In_Crossing: robot is physically within the railway crossing.
- Crossing_Clear: crossing is confirmed clear of the train.
- Sensor_Failure: one or more essential railway sensors have failed.
- Link_Lost: required railway communication link is lost.
- Chamber_Door_Open: chamber door is open.
- Robot_Delivering: robot is performing a delivery.
- Safe_Mode: robot is in protective mode.
- Sensors_Valid: relevant safety sensors are healthy and their readings are valid.
The constraints are evaluated using validated, current observations. A stale or unknown signal must not be treated as proof of safety.
Constraint ID	Constraint in simple English	Why it is necessary
C-01	If a train is present or approaching, the barrier must not be open.	Prevents train–robot collisions.
C-02	The robot may enter the crossing only if the barrier is confirmed open, no train is approaching, and safety sensors are valid.	Prevents unsafe crossing entry.
C-03	The barrier must remain closed while a train is passing.	Protects the crossing during train movement.
C-04	The barrier may open after train passage only when the crossing is confirmed clear and sensor readings are valid.	Prevents premature opening.
C-05	If an essential railway sensor fails, the robot must not enter the crossing and the system must raise an alert.	Prevents reliance on incorrect or missing readings.
C-06	If the railway communication link is lost, the robot must not begin crossing unless a valid, independently approved local safety policy confirms it is safe.	Prevents unsafe decisions based on stale information.
C-07	The robot must not deliver while the chamber door is open.	Prevents unsafe handling of temperature-sensitive medicines.
C-08	The robot must not deliver when the chamber is in protection mode or its required environmental conditions are unverified.	Protects medicine quality.
C-09	The robot must enter Safe Mode within 3 seconds of a defined critical battery or chamber/battery temperature trigger.	Enforces CR-01.
C-10	Significant vibration must suspend risky chamber activities until vibration stays below the threshold for the required stabilisation period.	Prevents damage to sensitive medicines and unreliable readings.
5.2 Formalisation of constraints
The symbols mean:
- \(\land\) = AND
- \(\lor\) = OR
- \(\neg\) = NOT
- \(\rightarrow\) = implies / if … then
Constraint ID	Formal expression
C-01	\(Train\_Present \rightarrow \neg Barrier\_Open\)
C-02	\(Robot\_Enter \rightarrow (Barrier\_Open \land \neg Train\_Present \land Sensors\_Valid)\)
C-03	\(Train\_Passing \rightarrow Barrier\_Closed\)
C-04	\(Barrier\_Open \rightarrow (Crossing\_Clear \land Sensors\_Valid)\)
C-05	\(Sensor\_Failure \rightarrow (\neg Robot\_Enter \land Alert)\)
C-06	\(Link\_Lost \rightarrow \neg Robot\_Enter\)
C-07	\(Chamber\_Door\_Open \rightarrow \neg Robot\_Delivering\)
C-08	\((Chamber\_Protection \lor \neg Environmental\_Conditions\_Verified) \rightarrow \neg Robot\_Delivering\)
C-09	\(Critical\_Trigger \rightarrow (Safe\_Mode\_Within\_3s)\)
C-10	\(Vibration\_High \rightarrow \neg Risky\_Chamber\_Activity\)
Interpretation: C-02 is an entry guard. The robot cannot enter just because the barrier appears open; the no-train condition and valid sensors must also be established. C-04 additionally ensures that the barrier-open state is supported by a verified clear crossing.
In an actual implementation, C-01 and C-03 should be represented with appropriate time-aware safety logic and fail-safe handling, rather than relying on a single instantaneous sensor sample.
5.3 Eight realistic constraint violations
A violation occurs when the observed values make a constraint false. The system must detect the violation and perform the defined safe response.
Violation ID	Constraint	Variable values causing violation	Detection method	Expected response
V-01	C-01	Train_Present=TRUE, Barrier_Open=TRUE	Train sensor and barrier-position feedback conflict with safety rules.	Command/maintain barrier closure, raise alarm, stop robot entry, and log event.
V-02	C-02	Robot_Enter=TRUE, Barrier_Open=FALSE, Train_Present=FALSE, Sensors_Valid=TRUE	Crossing-entry guard detects a closed barrier.	Reject entry and stop the robot before the crossing.
V-03	C-03	Train_Passing=TRUE, Barrier_Closed=FALSE	Train sensor and barrier-position feedback.	Raise a critical alarm, command closure if possible, prevent robot entry, and activate railway safety procedures.
V-04	C-04	Barrier_Open=TRUE, Crossing_Clear=FALSE, Sensors_Valid=TRUE	Crossing-clearance sensor disagrees with the open-barrier condition.	Close the barrier if safe and technically possible, alert the operator, and log the incident.
V-05	C-05	Sensor_Failure=TRUE, Robot_Enter=TRUE, Alert=FALSE	Sensor diagnostics and entry-monitor checks.	Block crossing entry, enter the railway system's protective mode, alert the operator, and log the failure.
V-06	C-06	Link_Lost=TRUE, Robot_Enter=TRUE	Link heartbeat timeout and crossing-entry monitor.	Stop entry, apply the local safe-response policy, and record the communication loss.
V-07	C-07	Chamber_Door_Open=TRUE, Robot_Delivering=TRUE	Door sensor and delivery-state monitor.	Suspend delivery, secure the batch, alert the operator, and log the violation.
V-08	C-08	Chamber_Protection=TRUE, Robot_Delivering=TRUE, Environmental_Conditions_Verified=FALSE	Chamber-state and environmental-condition monitor.	Stop delivery, maintain protection measures, notify Mission Control, and log the event.
Additional timing and vibration violation examples
Violation ID	Constraint	Variable values	Expected response
V-09	C-09	Critical_Trigger=TRUE, Safe_Mode_Within_3s=FALSE	Flag a critical timing failure, activate the available independent protective mechanism, alert Mission Control, and log the missed deadline.
V-10	C-10	Vibration_High=TRUE, Risky_Chamber_Activity=TRUE	Suspend risky activities, alert the operator if necessary, and resume only after the vibration remains below threshold for the stabilisation period.
These examples provide observable inputs and expected outcomes, so each violation can be reproduced during testing.
5.4 How the system should detect violations
The combined SACDCS and ARLCCS design should use three levels of checking:
1. Preventive checks: Before accepting an action, check whether the preconditions are satisfied. For example, the robot cannot enter a crossing while the barrier is closed.
2. Continuous monitoring: Monitor train detection, barrier position, communication health, chamber door status, temperature, humidity, and vibration during operation.
3. Fault response and logging: If a violation or critical fault is detected, execute the appropriate protective action, alert Mission Control, and record the observed values and timestamps.
An alarm alone is not sufficient when a physical hazard exists. The controller must also prevent or stop the unsafe action wherever technically possible.

